A||N||5||SloPonti||to rebuild trans or not to rebuild??||||5||
Z||000000||SloPonti||12-08-1999||12:40 AM||||i am rebuilding my motor in my 84 coupe and am almost ready to put the thing back together! my transmission is working fine but should i have it looked at! the local trans shop said it would cost 300.00 to go through and replace all seals and gaskets and the trans kit would cost me 100.00! so a total of 400.00! there is a 100,000 miles on the trans but like i said it runs fine! should i spend the money and do this or should i just put it back in the car?<BR>thanks||||reg||5
Z||000001||Monkeyman||12-08-1999||03:14 AM||||My tranny leaks somewhere.  I called the local dealership and this is what they told me. (BTW-I have 99,400mi on mine)  They can check it out for $40.  They can replace the seals on the outside for about $50.  If it shifts fine (I assume you have an auto), leave it alone.  I don't need a shift kit.  (I've heard differently, but this is what they said.)  You can buy the shift kit through the Fiero Store (I think) for about $50 and, supposedly you can install it yourself.  Just remove the pan and it goes in there.  I haven't tried this, but that's what the dealership said.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DJRice||12-08-1999||08:40 AM||||I have always heard with Transmissions, that if they arent broke, dont mess with them.  If its an auto, then it wont hurt to drain it and replace the filter and fluid, but other than that I would leave it be.||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||12-08-1999||10:27 AM||||If you have the tranny out, go ahead and replace all the seals too. Cheap insurance! Nothing worse than having to drop the cradle again in 6 months just because a tranny seal began leaking.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierospeeder||12-08-1999||12:17 PM||||If i had the money i would get it rebuilt. When you bought the car, did the previous owner change the trans fluid?  Most trans mechanics says that when you change the original fluid thats been in the trans since the car was bought brand new.  The new fluid will be thinner making the parts have greater tolerances then before.  Theres a chance that the trans will behave differently.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||12-08-1999||07:20 PM||||I had a bit of time this eve I wasn't expecting.<P>If the tranny has been running fine leave it alone with the following exceptions.<P>1. remove the pan and replace the filter. The kit for this is less than 20$. Tip: most kits come with updated filters. put filter seal into the tranny hole then push the filter into the seal. you may also have to bend the support clip to clear the new type filter. The pan will not fit if the new filter is used without bending the support clip.<P>2. Drain everything. replace the torque converter neck seal. (Pump seal it's also called) Use the new Dexron 3 when refilling.<P>3. Skip the shift kit. You'll be suprised what fresh oil, especially the new Dexron3 does for shifting.||||reg||1
